1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
2: """mvme187memcoob -- DETECTOR (built on its rung-3 witness) for the MVME187 memory controller, dev_mvme187.c.
3: 
4: DEFECT (filed 2026-10-02).  DEVICE_ACCESS(mvme187_memc) ended the emulator (fatal() + exit(1)) on a store to memconf (+0x08) and on any
5: offset its switch did not name, and indexed the 124-byte register struct with the folded offset (0..0xff) before the switch -- an
6: out-of-bounds read in the C source for 0x7c..0xff whose value the exiting arms never used.
7: 
8: THE FIX (#NNN).  Each served offset reads its own field (chipid +0x00, chiprev +0x04, memconf +0x08); controller 1 (+0x100..0x1ff)
9: folds onto the same image; a memconf store is not kept and is announced once per device; any other offset reads 0, a store there is
10: not kept, and the first such access is announced once per device with its direction, offset, controller and (for a store) value.  No
11: indexed read remains: the three served arms read their own fields and the default arm reads none.
12: 
13: WHAT THE ROWS MEASURE.  Cold `-E mvme88k -e mvme187 -M 64` debugger runs (UNMODIFIED in-tree) with r3 = 0xfff43000 (MVME187_MEM_CTLR).
14: The witness rows place one m88k ld.bu / st.b with `put w` and step it once.  The controls read chipid, chiprev, memconf and controller
15: 1's chipid: their session and pc rows, with `neg apparatus`, are the nine APPARATUS PINS (the CONTROL rows), and their r2 rows pin the
16: per-field reads.  The four sessions the old code ended (loads of the unserved offsets +0x0c, +0x7c and +0xff, and a memconf store) must
17: survive to the prompt.  The latch rows need several accesses in ONE emulator lifetime, so msession() places each word at the next word
18: from 0x10000, steps each once, sends each load to its own register (r2, r4-r15, each seeded with 0x55555555 so a load that does not
19: retire cannot read as a green 0) and reads them all at the end.  R3: a memconf store of 0xff, the memconf load (3: no bit kept), a
20: load at +0x7c (0), a controller-1 memconf store; exactly two announce lines, one per category, none naming the second store.  R4: the
21: chipid and chiprev loads, then four unserved-offset loads, the last through controller 1; the served loads read their fields, the
22: rest read 0, pc advances, one announce naming offset 0xc.  R5: a default-arm store, the load back (0), an aliased store; one announce
23: carrying the value.  R6: the six served reads through both controllers; no announce.  R7: through controller 1, a default load, a
24: memconf load (3) and a memconf store; two announces, both naming controller 1.  R8: a memconf store and a default load set both
25: latches, then the twelve bytes +0x7c..+0x87 are read one by one; every one reads 0.  Every notice fragment a row matches runs to the
26: notice's closing " (once) ]".  Row names are at most 16 characters (the gate's two-space anchor).  The multi-access sessions are fifteen:
27: R3 to R17.  msession() reads r2 and r4..r31 (R16 sends loads up to r27).
28: 
29: REPAIR 5 (p4_20261105/CONS_MYTHOS_local.md section 4; 148 -> 206 rows).  The two announce latches (M = warned_memconf, D =
30: warned_default) are observed only at memconf stores and default accesses, so the latch states form four phases (M, D) = 00, 10,
31: 01, 11.  R16 (memconf first: phases 00 -> 10 -> 11) and R17 (a default read at +0xf3 first: phase 01) make, in each of their
32: phases, served byte stores of 0x00 and 0xff with one readback per field per controller and memconf reads through both controllers, with a first
33: memconf store of 0x07 (controller 0), a controller-1 memconf store of 0x00 and a first default WORD store of 0xffffffff between
34: R16's phases; R5, R7, R9, R12, R13 and R15 gain tails: a second memconf store after a first store of 0x07 (R7, R15) and of
35: 0xffffffff (R12), a second default access after a first read at 0x0c (R12) and at 0x10c (R7) and after a first word store at 0x118
36: (R13), a first memconf store of 0x07 made with the default latch set (R5, R13), a first default read through controller 1 after
37: controller-1 memconf stores (R9), a first default word store with the memconf latch set (R15), and memconf read back after every
38: 0x07 store.  Wants are the ruling's derivation.  These rows are COVERAGE: the closure CONS section 3 claimed for them (every access
39: kind in every phase, both latches and the three fields read after each) is not what they do -- no controller-0 memconf store occurs
40: in phase 10 (R16's phase-10 memconf store is ST_B(0,3,0x108), controller 1), no controller-1 default store occurs in phase 11, and
41: no chipid or chiprev readback follows R16's phase-11 tail (adj_p5_20261105/ADJ_MYTHOS_local.md, R1).
42: 
43: CONTRACT (adj_p5_20261105/ADJ_MYTHOS_local.md section 2, checked line by line against PRETABLE_5.out's survivor summary at
44: :9460-9697 and tightened where the summary contradicted it: r6_20261105/RECORDS6_NOTE.md).  The FLOOR of this fix is each repair
45: path's own guard crossed with the state that path reads: the memconf path's {write, !M} x {memconf, M}; the default path's {default
46: arm, !D} x {D}; each served arm x its own field.  `controller`, len, idata and the offset bits are NOT floor predicates: the fold
